  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1253 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThe remnants of tropical cyclone Isaias, which is predicted to make \nlandfall along the central Gulf coast sometime around Friday night, \nwill drift northward up the Mississippi River valley on Saturday and \nSaturday night, before shunting off to the east on Sunday. As it \napproaches the midwest, its primary effects including clouds and \nrain will remain southeast of our area, but it will pinch off the \nweak 500 MB ridging in place prior to its passage, resulting in \nlighter mid-level flow over Iowa. It may also cause a slackening of \nthe low-level pressure gradient and winds in our southeastern \ncounties on Saturday. Our northwestern counties, however, will \nreside closer to the broad surface low pressure trough slowly \napproaching from the High Plains, and within a tighter pressure \ngradient resulting in stronger winds in those areas. This will be \nenhanced by deeper surface-layer mixing evidenced by forecast \nsoundings, which support peak gusts in the 35-40 KT range around \nEstherville and farther north and west. Have maintained a forecast \nin that range, and also trended dewpoints downward by several \ndegrees given the efficient mixing anticipated as well as recent \nmodel tendencies for overforecasting daytime moisture. The \ncombination of gusty winds and relatively low RH may support some \nelevated fire weather concerns Saturday in areas with cured fuels, \nnamely most croplands. This concern will be monitored over the next \nfew days.\n\nAs the remnants of Isaias move eastward away from Iowa on Sunday, \nthe surface trough will continue to slowly approach and the pressure \ngradient and mixing depth will decrease, leading to more modest \nsouth southwest breezes and slightly less warm temperatures Sunday \nafternoon, but still peaking in the upper 70s. By Monday morning, \nanother pattern change will be underway as a 500 MB low develops \nnear the Pacific coast of southern California, while a northern \nstream 500 MB trough moves quickly near the central U.S./Canada \nborder. As the latter feature scoots by to our north it will spread \nclouds and rain across the Dakotas, Minnesota, and Wisconsin that \nmay just scrape northern Iowa where low (20-30%) POPs are \nmaintained. Of possibly greater sensible weather impact is that the \npassage of this mid-level trough will also cause the surface trough, \nwhich had been creeping over the High Plains for several preceding \ndays, to surge quickly southeastward through Iowa during the day and \ninto Monday evening. This will bring brisk northerly breezes Monday \nnight followed by a quick passage of surface high pressure on \nTuesday. A somewhat more active weather pattern is expected\nthereafter as the southwestern U.S. 500 MB low evolves, however,\nmodel depictions of this evolution and particularly the degree\nof phasing between that system and the northern stream is quite\nerratic at this range, and will leave resolution of any details\nfor the coming days.",
